  "answer_core": "I would not grant Marcus 15% equity, a CTO title, and a board seat on Friday. Those are irreversible commitments, and right now several critical dependencies run through him at once: the platform prototype, technical delivery, Jake and Lina, client confidence, and part of the exit story. Granting the full package today would price his contribution and concentrate risk before you know whether he reduces it or deepens it.\n\nFriday should be a direct, structured conversation about a staged partnership, not a vague delay or a flat no. A vague “later” can read as avoidance and trigger exactly what you are trying to prevent: Marcus disengaging, pulling Jake and Lina with him, slowing the platform, or destabilizing client continuity.\n\nYour position should be that there is a credible path to major upside and leadership for him, but only after evidence. Define a short, specific sprint to test whether he can deliver the platform work, whether that work translates to revenue relevance, whether team continuity improves rather than worsens, whether knowledge transfer happens, and whether his behavior fits the governance you need. Keep intermediate structures alive if they help, but do not let the conversation turn into a catalog of instruments. Spell out what demonstrated success in that window would unlock.\n\nUse exit math only as a caution against false precision. Until buyer assumptions, platform value, and departure scenarios are tested, the numbers should not carry the decision. Go in ready to say you want to explore a serious path, but you cannot make irreversible governance and ownership commitments before proving what the partnership actually does for the company.",
